1
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:11,370
In the lap of the Sahyadris, dawn settles on the Vashishti river.

2
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:20,470
A sixty-three-year-old lawyer stands before the sun, as he does every day.

3
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,197
Om Suryaya Namah.

4
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,670
His name is Bhaskar Vishnu Khare. And this story... never happened.

5
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:36,580
But imagine — what if it had?

6
00:00:51,430 --> 00:00:57,550
Bombay, 1916. In the High Court, Lokmanya Tilak faces a charge of sedition.

7
00:00:59,330 --> 00:01:04,340
Rising in his defence is a young barrister of Bombay — Mohammad Ali Jinnah.

8
00:01:04,790 --> 00:01:08,620
That day, a lawyer was a patriot's shield.

9
00:01:10,670 --> 00:01:16,060
Thirty-one years later, at that same barrister's insistence, a map cracked in two.

10
00:01:26,370 --> 00:01:30,860
The Khare family of Chiplun. Sangh volunteers for four generations.

11
00:01:35,170 --> 00:01:40,000
In 1948 the house burned and the fields were lost — but not their resolve.

12
00:01:42,970 --> 00:01:51,130
A son of that house: Bhaskar. Government Law College, Mumbai. Then a small chamber in a narrow lane of Fort.

13
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:59,294
The law is not a sword. The law is a thread — it stitches what is torn.

14
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:06,390
At a lecture in that same college, he met Fatima Hussain.

15
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:13,280
Born in Uttar Pradesh. She walked out of an orthodox marriage and became a lawyer on her own strength.

16
00:02:14,530 --> 00:02:20,640
Mr Khare, you are a Sangh man. Will you argue with me, or have tea?

17
00:02:20,990 --> 00:02:23,443
Both. Tea first.

18
00:02:30,593 --> 00:02:36,773
In Persia, Islam adapted to Firdowsi. In Java, to the Ramayana.

19
00:02:37,223 --> 00:02:42,023
Then in Bharat too, the theology must bend — not the soil.

20
00:02:43,573 --> 00:02:45,861
And who are your ideals?

21
00:02:46,211 --> 00:02:53,251
Justice Chagla. Dr Kalam. Param Vir Abdul Hamid. Ustad Zakir Hussain.

22
00:02:53,601 --> 00:02:58,171
No one ever had to ask them — faith first, or country first.

23
00:02:59,621 --> 00:03:02,291
Then give this idea a name.

24
00:03:02,641 --> 00:03:06,291
Islam-e-Bharati. And what will you do?

25
00:03:06,641 --> 00:03:08,929
I will write its constitution.

26
00:03:11,021 --> 00:03:20,081
That evening, between two cups of tea, a movement was born — Islam-e-Bharati. And a small party — the MNP.

27
00:03:24,330 --> 00:03:26,820
The next thirty years were not easy.

28
00:03:30,730 --> 00:03:36,110
Fatwas came for Fatima. Boycotts. Empty chairs at her meetings.

29
00:03:45,130 --> 00:03:50,290
Call me what you like. But do not stop the daughters from going to school.

30
00:03:53,730 --> 00:04:02,720
And Bhaskar? He kept writing. The party's constitution. Drafts of reform. Clause by clause, night by night.

31
00:04:04,730 --> 00:04:08,781
Slogans last a day. Clauses last a hundred years.

32
00:04:11,930 --> 00:04:16,400
Meanwhile the world was changing. America turned inward.

33
00:04:21,930 --> 00:04:27,100
India and the European Union signed a historic trade deal.

34
00:04:31,930 --> 00:04:36,420
Saudi Arabia and Turkey cut off funding to radical groups.

35
00:04:41,930 --> 00:04:44,190
And then came that election night.

36
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:47,579
A hung parliament! No one has a majority.

37
00:04:57,730 --> 00:05:07,180
The key to government lay with just three seats. Mumbai. Western UP. West Bengal. All three — the MNP's.

38
00:05:09,930 --> 00:05:16,146
We have one condition. Keep your banner — the Prime Minister is ours.

39
00:05:18,196 --> 00:05:23,446
The NDA kept its banner. And the Prime Minister was — Fatima Hussain.

40
00:05:23,996 --> 00:05:32,216
I, Fatima Hussain, do swear that I will bear true faith and allegiance to the Constitution of India.

41
00:05:34,266 --> 00:05:41,156
And Bhaskar Vishnu Khare became Principal Legal Advisor. As always, one step behind.

42
00:05:44,597 --> 00:05:48,507
Across the border, Pakistan's economy collapsed.

43
00:05:52,597 --> 00:05:55,607
Queues for flour. Cities gone dark.

44
00:06:00,997 --> 00:06:05,137
People took to the streets. The military regime crushed them.

45
00:06:10,797 --> 00:06:13,817
And then — a terror strike in India.

46
00:06:19,197 --> 00:06:22,645
The nation is stunned. The whole country is demanding an answer.

47
00:06:26,997 --> 00:06:30,165
Prime Minister, the country wants revenge.

48
00:06:30,815 --> 00:06:33,075
Not revenge. Change.

49
00:06:36,597 --> 00:06:42,576
Occupiers create martyrs; constitutions create citizens.

50
00:06:46,397 --> 00:06:49,937
We will not take land. We will break a chain.

51
00:06:53,297 --> 00:06:57,657
Seventy-two hours. Precision strikes — on military targets only.

52
00:07:04,897 --> 00:07:06,857
A cyber campaign froze the command.

53
00:07:12,897 --> 00:07:15,647
The nuclear sites were secured.

54
00:07:21,097 --> 00:07:25,667
And then the Prime Minister spoke to the people across the border — in Urdu.

55
00:07:29,297 --> 00:07:31,687
My Pakistani sisters and brothers.

56
00:07:32,237 --> 00:07:39,867
This war is not with you. It is with those who took your bread, and put a gun in your children's hands.

57
00:07:41,797 --> 00:07:47,057
We do not want your land. We will not touch your faith.

58
00:07:47,507 --> 00:07:51,697
Your soil is yours. Your constitution, you will write yourselves.

59
00:07:53,897 --> 00:07:56,837
Rise. Take your country back.

60
00:08:01,897 --> 00:08:08,687
That night, from Lahore to Karachi, people walked toward the garrisons. Unarmed. In their hundreds of thousands.

61
00:08:16,297 --> 00:08:21,617
By morning the junta had laid down its arms. Not one city was occupied.

62
00:08:29,097 --> 00:08:34,897
But there was one more power — China. It had billions of dollars in CPEC and Gwadar.

63
00:08:39,097 --> 00:08:41,787
Its troops began to mass at the border.

64
00:08:47,297 --> 00:08:48,547
Bhaskar — now what?

65
00:08:49,097 --> 00:08:52,952
Not bullets now, Madam. The ledger.

66
00:08:55,202 --> 00:08:58,622
Behind the curtain, Bhaskar played three cards.

67
00:09:01,702 --> 00:09:09,422
First — trade. China's large trade surplus with India. In a war, that market closes.

68
00:09:13,702 --> 00:09:20,712
Second — a covenant with Balochistan. Autonomy, and royalties on its own mineral wealth.

69
00:09:25,702 --> 00:09:32,162
Third — the debt. Every rupee owed to China would be repaid. In instalments, with guarantees.

70
00:09:36,902 --> 00:09:46,871
Do you want a port, or your money? You will get the money. The port stays for trade — not for any army.

71
00:09:49,721 --> 00:09:55,011
Gwadar became a commercial port. And China's troops went home.

72
00:10:01,121 --> 00:10:08,191
And then, on the bank of the Indus — the leaders of India, Pakistan and Bangladesh, at one table.

73
00:10:12,321 --> 00:10:20,481
Three nations. Three flags. Three constitutions. And one shared union — open borders, a common market.

74
00:10:24,321 --> 00:10:27,031
The preamble to the treaty was written by Bhaskar.

75
00:10:27,681 --> 00:10:40,629
A barrister from Bombay once severed our unity; an advocate from Bombay, bearing the flame of Tilak, establishes that the soil binds us all.

76
00:10:43,679 --> 00:10:49,159
Faith bends to the soil. Not the soil to faith.

77
00:10:52,479 --> 00:10:55,319
It was named — the Sindhu Accord.

78
00:11:00,663 --> 00:11:04,683
That night, from Gilgit to Garo, the sky lit up.

79
00:11:10,463 --> 00:11:15,343
Bhaskar did not celebrate. He took the night train — home.

80
00:11:19,663 --> 00:11:25,483
And at dawn, by the Vashishti. This time not alone — with his children.

81
00:11:26,333 --> 00:11:27,930
Om Suryaya Namah.

